I added one process-level test that checks the whole --print stdout contract in a single run. It passes on the current code without any source change. Committed as d454f4aa; the only file touched is tests/print_stdout_contract.rs.

The scenario. The binary runs against the local SSE stub in a tempdir cwd with a tempdir HOME and no config:
• **Turn 1:** a thinking block, then a real read_file call on a.txt in the tempdir.
• **Turn 2:** another thinking block, a text delta that is exactly `"\n\n"`, then the answer. The answer contains a blank line in the middle, which is the near-miss: the strip must remove leading blank lines only.

What the two new tests check:
• `print_mode_whole_contract_stdout_is_exactly_the_answer`: stdout must be byte-for-byte the answer, with nothing trimmed before comparing. It also checks the tool's progress line reached stderr and that the stub received at least 2 requests, so the run really went through the tool turn.
• `json_mode_whole_contract_stdout_is_exactly_one_value: the first byte of stdout must be {, stdout must parse as exactly one JSON value, only the single newline println! adds may follow it, and response` must equal the answer with its middle blank line intact.
• The fixture checks itself before running, so a green result can't come from a stub that skipped one of the triggers.

Positive controls. Each was run as one mutate→run→restore command, one after the other, and afterwards git diff src/ was empty and there are no NEUTERED markers left:
• Disabling the leading-blank-line strip (answer_payload in src/main.rs) made both new tests fail by name, plus the existing leading-blank-line test.
• Disabling the reserve check in write_chrome in src/prompt.rs made both new tests fail by name, plus the two existing tool-turn tests.

Two corrections to the task text:
• The leading-newline strip lives in src/main.rs (answer_payload), not in src/prompt.rs.
• There is no trailing newline in --print mode. It uses print!, and the existing byte-exact tests already assert stdout equals the answer with nothing after it, so the new test does the same. The JSON envelope is the one that ends with a single \n.

Not covered by the new test: --help text (no model turn happens) and the stream that dies mid-answer (the run exits non-zero). The doc comment says so, and both keep their own tests.

cargo fmt --check, cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass.
